&lt;p&gt;Amazon fires&amp;mdash;whether uncontrolled wildfires or intentional burns&amp;mdash;produce smoke waves that can blanket the region with unhealthy air for months at a time.These smoke plumes are visible from space and affect the health and wellbeing of millions of Amazonians, yet there is little ground-based data on air quality outside major cities. In this talk, Marcia Macedo will share recent experiences building a ground-based network of low-cost sensors to monitor particulate matter (PM 2.5 ) at &amp;ldquo;nose level&amp;rdquo; over large swaths of the Amazon.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This sensor network, which began as a local project in the state of Acre, Brazil, has grown into a regional grassroots movement that includes Indigenous communities and associations, local universities, civil society partners, and municipal governments. Together, they have deployed hundreds of commercially available low-cost sensors to help fill the large gaps in air quality data for rural areas. This talk will share the story of how this coalition came to be &amp;ndash; outlining the technical challenges of maintaining a reliable monitoring network in humid, infrastructure-limited environments, while highlighting emerging opportunities to leverage the data collected to support public health policies and improve fire management.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Speaker&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://e3b.columbia.edu/content/marcia-macedo" target="_blank"&gt;Marcia Macedo&lt;/a&gt;, Assistant Professor of Ecology, Evolution and Environmental Biology at Columbia University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Information&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free and open to the public; &lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSd--sVeU1jGM0b55hnBNQWjNkqMZ31S-t3L0t57bbKH80H8rA/viewform?usp=header" target="_blank"&gt;registration&lt;/a&gt; required. Contact &lt;a href="mailto:scienceandsociety@columbia.edu"&gt;scienceandsociety@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; with any questions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted by the &lt;a href="https://scienceandsociety.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Center for Science and Society&lt;/a&gt; at Columbia University.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Presentation by&amp;nbsp;Xiyuan Bao.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deep Provenance of East Africa and Indian Ocean Hotspots through Tomography, Graph Theory, and Geochemical Cross-validation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hotspot volcanism is conventionally attributed to vertical mantle plumes from deep mantle sources to the surface. However, recent seismic imaging reveals complex networks of plume-like anomalies, making surface-source mapping challenging due to possible non-uniqueness. We propose an objective, graph-theory-based framework that constructs an ensemble of plume paths using a high-resolution regional seismic tomographic model. Applied to eight East African and Indian Ocean hotspots, our method consistently identifies clustered source zones near the core&amp;ndash;mantle boundary in different regional/global tomographic models, showing robust grouping of Afar, East Africa, and Comores, versus Reunion, versus Marion, Crozet, Kerguelen and Amsterdam-St Paul. Multivariate analysis with machine learning of the Sr-Nd-Pb isotope ratios of hotspot-related basalts reveals the same clustering. Hence, we infer the first deep mantle compositional zoning map in this region, delineating at least three distinct domains within the African Large Low Shear Velocity Province (LLSVP).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Making&amp;nbsp;informed&amp;nbsp;choices&amp;nbsp;about&amp;nbsp;how&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;use&amp;nbsp;your&amp;nbsp;time&amp;nbsp;can&amp;nbsp;set&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;stage&amp;nbsp;for&amp;nbsp;academic&amp;nbsp;success. Participants will discuss factors that impact how they choose to spend their time, managing time effectively, building a balanced schedule, and learn about tools for overcoming procrastination.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Session&amp;nbsp;open&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;all&amp;nbsp;Columbia&amp;nbsp;University&amp;nbsp;students,&amp;nbsp;regardless&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;school&amp;nbsp;affiliation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note:&amp;nbsp;This&amp;nbsp;session&amp;nbsp;will&amp;nbsp;not&amp;nbsp;be&amp;nbsp;recorded,&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;slides&amp;nbsp;are&amp;nbsp;not&amp;nbsp;shared.&amp;nbsp;If you&amp;#39;re no longer able to attend, please remove your registration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on February 16, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Global democracy was in crisis before Donald Trump returned to the White House, but the dismantling of U.S. foreign aid and other democracy-building &amp;ldquo;soft power&amp;rdquo; projects is certain to deepen that crisis. Decades of democracy promotion efforts have now vanished or been greatly diminished In Russia, Eurasia, and Eastern Europe, removing a vital counterweight to Russian disinformation and influence efforts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This historic change, and its impact on democracy in the region, is examined in detail in the 2026 issue of &amp;quot;Harriman Magazine.&amp;quot; Join magazine authors &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/timothy-m-frye/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Timothy Frye&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/thomas-kent/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thomas Kent&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and editor-in-chief &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/ann-cooper/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ann Cooper&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for a discussion of soft power and democracy promotion in the region &amp;ndash; its history and what happens now that it&amp;rsquo;s been abolished. Moderated by editor &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/masha-udensiva-brenner/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Masha Udensiva-Brenner&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Please email&amp;nbsp;disability@columbia.edu&amp;nbsp;to request disability accommodations. Advance notice is necessary to arrange for some accessibility needs.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Our Fellows&amp;rsquo; seminars are now open to the public!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fascinating topics from the humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event has&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/directory/alessia-zambon"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Alessia&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/directory/alessia-zambon"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Zambon&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;in conversation with moderator&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://history.columbia.edu/person/eldem-edhem/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edhem Eldem&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Visiting Professor in the Department of History, Columbia University.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Alessia Zambon&lt;/strong&gt;, of the University of Versailles Saint-Quentin-en-Yvelines (UVSQ; France) joins the Italian Academy as a Spring semester Fellow; her project is titled &amp;quot;Western travelers, local emissaries and ancient monuments in Ottoman Greece (1784&amp;ndash;1834).&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Zambon is senior lecturer in Art History and Cultural Heritage in the History Department of the University of Versailles Saint-Quentin-en-Yvelines (University Paris&amp;ndash;⁠Saclay). Her research focuses on the explorations and acquisitions of antiquities by western travelers and local emissaries in Southern Italy, the Ottoman Empire and Greece in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries and on the history of collections in Europe.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
She is the author of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Aux origines de l&amp;#39;arch&amp;eacute;ologie en Gr&amp;egrave;ce: Fauvel et sa m&amp;eacute;thode&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Paris, INHA-CTHS, 2014), a book resulting from her dissertation and focusing on the archaeological work of the painter Louis Fran&amp;ccedil;ois Fauvel (1753&amp;ndash;⁠1838) in Greece and the Ottoman Empire. She has been scholar associate at the Getty Villa (2024), visiting scholar at Columbia University (2022) and at the &amp;Eacute;cole fran&amp;ccedil;aise d&amp;#39;Ath&amp;egrave;nes (2019), associate member of the American School of Classical Studies at Athens (2011&amp;ndash;⁠2012), postdoctoral fellow at the Getty Research Institute in Los Angeles (2010&amp;ndash;⁠2011), and associate researcher at the D&amp;eacute;partement des Estampes et de la photographie of the Biblioth&amp;egrave;que Nationale de France (2008&amp;ndash;⁠2011). She has participated in several international research projects on the history of archaeology and the constitution of France&amp;#39;s ancient heritage. In 2022, she edited with Irini Apostolou the book&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Du pillage &amp;agrave; la conscience patrimoniale en Gr&amp;egrave;ce et dans l&amp;rsquo;Empire ottoman : le r&amp;ocirc;le des Fran&amp;ccedil;ais et des autres Occidentaux (XVIIIe&amp;ndash;⁠XIXe s.)&lt;/em&gt;, that questions how the looting of antiquities, carried out by Western countries between the eighteenth and early twentieth centuries, paved the way to the emergence of a heritage consciousness in Greece and the Ottoman Empire (&lt;a href="https://books.openedition.org/ausonius/22244" target="_blank"&gt;https://books.openedition.org/ausonius/22244&lt;/a&gt;). Her next book, edited with Vincent Puech and Delphine Carrangeot, will be published in 2026 with the title: L&lt;em&gt;es transferts d&amp;rsquo;antiquites: objets, collections, processus, patrimonialisatio&lt;/em&gt;n&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;(de l&amp;rsquo;Antiquite au XIXe s.)&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;More information:&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.ieci.uvsq.fr/mme-alessia-zambon" target="_blank"&gt;https://www.ieci.uvsq.fr/mme-alessia-zambon&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/events/western-travelers-ancient-monuments-ottoman-greece"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;to register.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Doors open at 3:30pm.&amp;nbsp;Registration does not guarantee a seat; registrants are seated first-come, first served.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
This event is in-person only.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calling all environmental humanities students, practitioners, and scholars! Join the Environmental Humanities Working Group. During monthly sessions, we will take deep dives into fictional and non-fiction texts, media pieces, and writings through discussions and shared activities. Occasionally when shared, prior engagement with focused meeting materials is encouraged, but not required.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During our first session, we&amp;rsquo;ll host an evening of networking, community building, and a short film screening followed by meaningful discussion and working group goal setting. Drinks and light refreshments provided.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Information&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free and open to Columbia University ID holders; &lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScULTA63Z8P8-Nvosll4RCtyfVktONHBbjCLX4H69Ypi_DM9A/viewform?usp=header" target="_blank"&gt;registration&lt;/a&gt; required. Contact &lt;a href="mailto:scienceandsociety@columbia.edu"&gt;scienceandsociety@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; with any questions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted by the Center for Science and Society and the &lt;a href="https://english.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Department of English and Comparative Literature&lt;/a&gt; at Columbia University.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To join our growing university community and stay up to date on environmental humanities working group news, updates, and activities, please sign up for our listserve via our &lt;a href="https://forms.gle/cXMrgUvkWdxtV4Q69" target="_blank"&gt;form&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on February 16, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a book talk by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Svetlana Satchkova&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-lipovetsky/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this darkly funny and emotionally resonant novel of contemporary Russia, a young filmmaker unexpectedly finds herself targeted by an authoritarian regime&amp;mdash;despite her best efforts to stay out of politics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;The Undead&amp;quot; is a layered and sharply observant portrait of an artist caught in the machinery of state power, and the choices one faces in a system where even indifference can be dangerous. Written with the intimate insight of an &amp;eacute;migr&amp;eacute; from Putin&amp;#39;s Russia, &amp;quot;The Undead&amp;quot; is both a compelling narrative and a chilling reflection of how repression operates today&amp;mdash;not just against dissidents, but against anyone.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When Maya, a young Russian filmmaker, makes a low-budget horror movie, it seems like a promising start to her indie film career. But her jokey lo-fi picture soon attracts the attention of the autocratic state, and Maya is swept into a nightmarish system where logic breaks down and no one is safe.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Please email&amp;nbsp;disability@columbia.edu&amp;nbsp;to request disability accommodations. Advance notice is necessary to arrange for some accessibility needs.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Brazilian-born guitarist and composer Chico Pinheiro&amp;mdash;hailed by&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Jazz Weekly&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;as &amp;ldquo;one of the most important and celebrated guitarists today&amp;rdquo;&amp;mdash;captivated Miller audiences last season in his unforgettable duo performance with vocalist Luciana Souza. Now, for his&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Pop-Up Concerts&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;series debut, he returns with an intimate set that blends Brazilian classics, jazz standards, and his own striking original compositions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Free admission &amp;bull; Doors open at 5:30PM, music at 6PM&lt;br /&gt;
Onstage seating is first-come, first-served.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sofia Alaoui, 2023, 91 min.&lt;br /&gt;
In French, Arabic and Berber with English subtitles.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Itto, a young woman from a modest rural background, is slowly adapting to the Moroccan privileged codes of her husband&amp;rsquo;s family. But when supernatural events put the country in a state of emergency, Itto finds herself separated from her husband and new family. Alone, pregnant and looking for her way back, she finds emancipation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Watch the trailer &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/N1L2pU_Osxs?si=7shoFoiMY__88cT1"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you want sleight-of-hand cinematic atmosphere, a big movie made on a small budget, and a sense of the planet tipping on its axis toward destruction and rebirth, here&amp;#39;s your vision of the end times. - Ty Burr of Substack (Top Critic)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Animalia&lt;/em&gt; is a quiet, thoughtful film with grand things on its mind that it tackles with impressive confidence, to great success. - Alexandra Heller-Nicholas of AWFJ (Top Critic)&lt;/p&gt;

